Great.

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:43.510
He's going to go back and work on it.

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:44.510
Feedback was given.

00:11:44.510 --> 00:11:46.910
I'm being proactive instead of being reactive.

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:49.910
You're just waiting for us to tell you what
to do and you're no longer being proactive.

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:51.550
You used to be, and you're taking a hit.

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:53.290
And quite frankly, this behavior cannot continue.

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:54.360
You're effecting the entire team.

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:55.360
Fair enough.

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:56.560
So you've got to have that conversation.

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:59.400
It's not just a one-way highway, a both-way
highway.

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:02.280
So if you want low salary, you want low profits,
we'll give you equity.

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:04.710
If you want equity, you're going to have this
[profits].

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:07.900
Or somebody says, well, I want a decent salary.

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:09.120
But I don't care about equity.

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:11.730
I want to participate in bonuses this year.

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:12.730
Okay.

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:15.620
So instead of getting a $40,000 salary, you
want a $50,000 year salary, but you want to

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:19.530
be able to bonus $20 grand this year.

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:20.530
No problem.

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:21.870
We can work that out.

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:22.980
But zero equity.

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:23.980
Great.

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:27.530
Which means that at any point, this person
can leave and go elsewhere, or this person

00:12:27.530 --> 00:12:35.500
may have some issues, tax liens, they may
have a unpaid child support payments that

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:37.170
they want to pay off.

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:41.340
Or they have certain debt they owe to somebody
that they just want to pay it off.

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:42.340
And they have to fix it now.

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:44.510
And you don't know the whole story, and they
don't want to open up and tell you about it,

00:12:44.510 --> 00:12:45.590
quite frankly.

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:46.700
That's fine.

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:48.890
They're motivated by the bonuses, right?

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:50.020
So red, there's no equity.

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:51.370
They don't care for equity.

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:52.370
Great.

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:56.450
This person [salary] may come and say, I want
high salary, I don't care about bonuses, I

00:12:56.450 --> 00:12:58.480
want to participate in equity.

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:01.360
So what's the difference between this [equity]
and this guy?

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:03.580
Because this guy wants equity, but he's getting
low salary.

00:13:03.580 --> 00:13:05.670
This is when it gets interesting.

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:14.480
So in the world of sports, there are salary
caps.

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:15.480
Let's just say it, right?

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:18.670
And you know, there's always this collective
bargaining agreement that they have in NFL

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:25.140
and NBA and all this stuff and they come together
and any time you hear when there is a what

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:28.190
do they call it, a lock out?

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:31.860
When no one's playing and they're renegotiating,
the collective bargaining agreement there's

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:36.230
a NBA lockout or something where they're not
playing.

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:39.090
Hey, they're not playing 30 games, okay?

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:43.110
Because we're renegotiating - there's strike,
and there's a lock out.

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:49.690
So sometimes, as an athlete, sports team,
you may be sitting there saying, well wait

00:13:49.690 --> 00:13:55.890
a minute, I'm the Cavs, LeBron's contract
is coming up with Miami, listen, let's pay

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:56.890
him.

00:13:56.890 --> 00:13:58.160
Who cares?

00:13:58.160 --> 00:13:59.160
Right?

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:04.620
Sometimes, you're the Lakers, Jerry Buss,
and you've got a Magic Johnson that's coming

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:09.110
down from Michigan State, and you know he's
going to be a player and Larry Byrd is coming

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:13.120
and he's going straight to Boston from Indiana
and you're bringing Magic Johnson who's a

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:16.640
rock star, you know, you've got to kind of
participate.

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:23.940
And Jerry Buss eventually gave Magic five
percent of the Lakers and put him as a VP

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:28.060
of Operations, just vice president and afterwards
some kind of role he played.

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:29.070
And so he got equity.

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:30.070
Why?

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:32.780
Because Jerry Buss locked in Magic Johnson.

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:34.630
Magic Johnson only played for one MBA team.

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:36.610
It was the Lakers.

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:37.610
Right?

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:39.840
Sometimes you've got to lock in a Kobe Bryant.

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:41.940
Sometimes you've got to lock in your players.

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:47.020
In the NFL, they call it franchise tag.

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:49.190
So a lot of times quarterbacks will get franchise
tag.

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:50.910
You can't leave them or trade or all that.

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:52.891
So the company pretty much has you.

00:14:52.891 --> 00:14:56.900
It's like saying a non-compete, that you can't
leave and go to another team.

00:14:56.900 --> 00:14:57.900
Great.

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:02.510
So why would you consider at a point of doing
salary and equity, at the same time, and both

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:04.050
high numbers?

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:07.160
Because that talent may be a solid talent
that's coming on board.

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:08.660
Now keep this in mind.

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:12.090
I had somebody that I brought on board that
had very, very strong talent.

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:13.220
But I had a very serious conversation.

00:15:13.220 --> 00:15:16.120
I said, Look, I can't afford a high salary.

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:19.460
I can't even afford to give you profits right
now, this year.

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:22.600
But I'll give you long-term opportunity here
[equity].

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:25.000
And I wanted to kind of read the person and
the person agreed.

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:26.530
And they came on board.

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:32.420
And then from there on, the simple question
that I ask is, this person, how much value

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:33.980
did they increase of the company?

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:34.990
Think about it.

00:15:34.990 --> 00:15:40.820
So the moment LeBron James went to the Cleveland
Cavaliers, this sport franchise went up $300

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:42.100
million.

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:46.400
So you're paying LeBron $30 million a year,
LeBron increased the value of your team by

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:47.400
$300 million.

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:48.400
Was it worth it?

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:49.400
Think about that for a moment.

00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:55.130
The moment LeBron left Cleveland the first
time, they lost $300 million, in value.

00:15:55.130 --> 00:15:57.440
So this is numbers.

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:00.440
See, when I come, I'm bringing value to you.

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:04.850
So sometimes in the marketplace, when a Sheryl
Sandberg is on the market, and she's a free

00:16:04.850 --> 00:16:15.040
agent, and Mark Zuckerberg wants her, he brings
Sheryl Sandberg to handle the operations side,

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:18.780
the COO to start making money and all this
other stuff for him.

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:20.570
Sheryl Sandberg today is worth $1.5 billion.

00:16:20.570 --> 00:16:22.430
Do you know what her salary was?

00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:25.470
Can you take a wild guess what her salary
was when she first came to Facebook.

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:27.290
I bet you won't get it right.

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:28.380
It's not $10 million.

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:29.480
It's not $5 million.

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:30.490
It's not a million.

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:31.810
It's not a half a million.

00:16:31.810 --> 00:16:34.070
Her first salary was $300,000.

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:35.600
$300,000.

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:39.480
But she got stocks, $30 million.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:41.050
I want equity.

00:16:41.050 --> 00:16:42.850
I want to be able to take this company to
the next level.

00:16:42.850 --> 00:16:45.590
She did, her $30 million turned into one and
a half billion dollars.

00:16:45.590 --> 00:16:47.410
Whatever the number is right now.

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:48.410
Right?

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:49.410
Why?

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:50.410
Because she understood, I don't care about
salary.

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:51.410
I don't care about profits.

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:52.790
I'm going for the big play.

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:56.311
So you're realizing sometimes in the market,
you have some talent that you got to work

00:16:56.311 --> 00:16:57.630
that out with.

00:16:57.630 --> 00:17:01.490
Now, for the most part, most companies don't
give sales people equity.

00:17:01.490 --> 00:17:03.200
I like to do it.

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:04.630
I like to do it.

00:17:04.630 --> 00:17:06.669
And we have a certain program that we do that
for.

00:17:06.669 --> 00:17:07.669
Why?

00:17:07.669 --> 00:17:13.110
Because I think sales, a great sales manager,
a sales leader, let me restate that, not necessarily

00:17:13.110 --> 00:17:18.879
sales manager, a great sales leader is very
hard to find.

00:17:18.879 --> 00:17:21.110
Very, very hard to find.

00:17:21.110 --> 00:17:23.350
And you've got to value your sales leaders.

00:17:23.350 --> 00:17:24.839
You've got to value your great sales leaders.

00:17:24.839 --> 00:17:26.360
You've got to value them.

00:17:26.360 --> 00:17:27.769
A sales person can come and go.

00:17:27.769 --> 00:17:29.120
A sales leader?

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:31.789
They know how to teach other people how to
become sales leaders themselves.

00:17:31.789 --> 00:17:33.399
It's very difficult to find that.

00:17:33.399 --> 00:17:36.870
So, this is an idea for to be thinking about
when you're hiring somebody.

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:37.870
How do I combine what?

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:41.899
And by the way, a lot of times when you're
looking at this, I'm going to my last points

00:17:41.899 --> 00:17:48.789
here with you, a lot of times when you're
going into this, you've got to know your own

00:17:48.789 --> 00:17:49.789
numbers.

00:17:49.789 --> 00:17:55.450
For instance, if I'm giving equity to somebody,
I'm giving equity to someone that's irreplaceable.

00:17:55.450 --> 00:17:59.749
If you're watching this yourself, and you
are on the hiring end, so you're the employer,

00:17:59.749 --> 00:18:04.789
and you're thinking about hiring an employee
or promotion or hourly or equity or an executive,

00:18:04.789 --> 00:18:10.009
or a sales person, you've got to ask yourself,
if this person irreplaceable?

00:18:10.009 --> 00:18:11.009
So what is irreplaceable?

00:18:11.009 --> 00:18:13.510
They have certain relationships that you need.

00:18:13.510 --> 00:18:16.669
There are certain contacts that you need them
to be with you long term.

00:18:16.669 --> 00:18:19.159
There are certain skill sets that they have
that you need them to be here.

00:18:19.159 --> 00:18:25.789
There are certain capital they're bringing
to the table that's a non-measurable capital.

00:18:25.789 --> 00:18:28.210
It's not necessarily money, but they increase
the value of the company.

00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:29.210
Right?

00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:31.360
You need to understand, that person is irreplaceable.

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:36.080
If you're on the complete opposite end, and
you're maybe an hourly or salary, and you're

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:39.230
kind of wondering what is your edge going
to be when you're asking for profits or equity,

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:43.789
well you've got to ask yourself the question,
are you irreplaceable?

00:18:43.789 --> 00:18:47.179
Because if you're irreplaceable, then it's
very easy.

00:18:47.179 --> 00:18:49.470
Value can be determined if you're irreplaceable.

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:51.850
If you're replaceable, what do you want to
have?

00:18:51.850 --> 00:18:52.850
You have no leverage.

00:18:52.850 --> 00:18:54.900
So you've got to make sure you're irreplaceable.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:19:00.059
I literally sit down with my team and I tell
them, you need to become irreplaceable, and

00:19:00.059 --> 00:19:04.610
you'll increase the value you have with me,
with the company, long-term with us working

00:19:04.610 --> 00:19:05.610
together.

00:19:05.610 --> 00:19:07.570
If you're not irreplaceable, what are we doing
here?

00:19:07.570 --> 00:19:08.570
What are we doing here?

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:10.450
You've got to be irreplaceable.

00:19:10.450 --> 00:19:12.269
So now, strategies.

00:19:12.269 --> 00:19:13.580
Strategies to be thinking about.

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:15.190
#1: Know what you can afford.

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:20.390
I had a guy ask me the other day, Pat, there's
this person I want to hire and man, he's so

00:19:20.390 --> 00:19:25.720
great, I love him, but he's got kids, he's
got a wife, and you know, at his last company

00:19:25.720 --> 00:19:28.570
they paid him 180 a year, but I know we've
got a great opportunity.

00:19:28.570 --> 00:19:32.180
I want to bring him on board, and you know,
his last company was a part of, when they

00:19:32.180 --> 00:19:37.549
were bought, he made a half a million dollars,
and he's coming to me, he would completely

00:19:37.549 --> 00:19:39.330
change the life of a company if he came on
board.

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:41.759
I said, Okay, how much did your company make
last year?

00:19:41.759 --> 00:19:43.740
Well, we grossed $1 million.

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:44.740
Okay.

00:19:44.740 --> 00:19:45.830
What'd you net last year?

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:46.830
$50,000.

00:19:46.830 --> 00:19:49.950
I said, what can you afford to pay this guy?

00:19:49.950 --> 00:19:51.059
I can afford $50,000.

00:19:51.059 --> 00:19:53.330
I said, you can afford $50,000?

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:55.509
I said, "What else can you give him?"

00:19:55.509 --> 00:19:56.509
That's it.

00:19:56.509 --> 00:19:57.700
I said, what's the projectory of your company?

00:19:57.700 --> 00:20:00.909
Did you go from $100,000 to half a million
to a million?

00:20:00.909 --> 00:20:01.909
No.

00:20:01.909 --> 00:20:02.909
What was the projectory?

00:20:02.909 --> 00:20:04.559
Have you been going like this?

00:20:04.559 --> 00:20:07.749
No, we've been going kind of like this.

00:20:07.749 --> 00:20:11.009
That's not a, a person to come and say to
go they're going from hundred , half a million,

00:20:11.009 --> 00:20:14.240
a million, two million, 10 million, 40 million,
this is great.

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:15.480
Right?

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:16.480
I want a piece of this.

00:20:16.480 --> 00:20:19.070
And three years my buy out's going to be $4
million.

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:20.070
I'm interested.

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:21.070
Right?

00:20:21.070 --> 00:20:23.590
But if it's like this [slow growth], you don't
have an argument to get that guy as a talent.

00:20:23.590 --> 00:20:25.789
He's going to say, "What are you talking about?"

00:20:25.789 --> 00:20:31.260
And you can't give him $50,000 while his expenses
, he's 36, 37 years old, he's got a wife,

00:20:31.260 --> 00:20:32.260
kids and a mortgage.

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:33.509
He's not going to do it at $50,000 a year.

00:20:33.509 --> 00:20:35.490
So you need to know your numbers.

00:20:35.490 --> 00:20:41.309
You may bring him in, on your advisory board,
and have him stick around on the advisory

00:20:41.309 --> 00:20:46.250
board, and you work your tail off so he sees
the projectory and you say, hey, you want

00:20:46.250 --> 00:20:47.250
to come and be the president?

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:48.419
Now I can afford 150.

00:20:48.419 --> 00:20:49.419
I'm coming in.

00:20:49.419 --> 00:20:51.090
Because he's fallen in love with the company
now.

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:52.090
Makes sense.

00:20:52.090 --> 00:20:53.090
That's a completely different story.

00:20:53.090 --> 00:20:54.409
You need to know your numbers.

00:20:54.409 --> 00:20:57.340
Two, always keep a look out for free agents.

00:20:57.340 --> 00:20:58.460
Always.

00:20:58.460 --> 00:20:59.529
Always keep a look out for free agents.

00:20:59.529 --> 00:21:02.149
We've got the insurance commission coming
up next week.

00:21:02.149 --> 00:21:06.080
I've got so many messages from people telling
me, Pat, you know, what can I do to be part

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:07.179
of a VP with your company?

00:21:07.179 --> 00:21:08.580
I want to work from the home office side.

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:12.480
I think I can bring value to your marketing
side or the operations side, all this other

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:13.480
stuff.

00:21:13.480 --> 00:21:15.070
I'm always looking for free agents.

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:17.450
I'm always looking for great free agents.

00:21:17.450 --> 00:21:19.950
I mean, amazing free agents.

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:20.950
Why?

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:26.009
Because free agents, it's free agents.

00:21:26.009 --> 00:21:29.860
When LeBron is on the trading block, you've
got to pick him up.

00:21:29.860 --> 00:21:31.779
This doesn't happen all the time.

00:21:31.779 --> 00:21:34.270
When he gets some killers on the trading blocks,
you've got to make sure you go pick those

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:35.320
guys up.

00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:39.970
So always keep a lookout for free agents in
the marketplace.

00:21:39.970 --> 00:21:42.441
#3, know the market for the position you're
hiring for.

00:21:42.441 --> 00:21:46.461
For instance, I had somebody that came to
me and said, well Patrick, you know, I want

00:21:46.461 --> 00:21:48.710
to one day make $300,000 a year with you.

00:21:48.710 --> 00:21:49.710
I love this guy.

00:21:49.710 --> 00:21:50.710
I said, "Really?"

00:21:50.710 --> 00:21:51.710
He said, yes.

00:21:51.710 --> 00:21:52.710
I said, that will never happen.

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:53.710
Why not?

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:58.120
Because the market for what you're doing,
is this.

00:21:58.120 --> 00:21:59.960
Oh, well, I'm going to go somewhere else.

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:00.960
I said, no problem.

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:03.190
Take the next two months, and you start looking.

00:22:03.190 --> 00:22:04.190
This is not today.

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:05.190
This is years ago.

00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:09.320
Take the next two months and start looking.

00:22:09.320 --> 00:22:10.909
And he goes looking.

00:22:10.909 --> 00:22:13.220
And he comes back, and I said, so what happened?

00:22:13.220 --> 00:22:15.030
Well, I got a lot of offers.

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:16.030
Great.

00:22:16.030 --> 00:22:17.030
Did anybody offer you 300?

00:22:17.030 --> 00:22:18.030
No.

00:22:18.030 --> 00:22:19.030
Oh, really?

00:22:19.030 --> 00:22:20.030
Yes.

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:21.030
So what's the number?

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:22.030
Well, you know.

00:22:22.030 --> 00:22:23.030
I said, I know the number for your position.

00:22:23.030 --> 00:22:25.249
And I just took the printout, this is your
number.

00:22:25.249 --> 00:22:27.100
This is exactly your number.

00:22:27.100 --> 00:22:29.240
This is exactly your number.

00:22:29.240 --> 00:22:30.710
Stuttering, stuttering.

00:22:30.710 --> 00:22:31.710
Did you talk to other people?

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:32.710
Yes.

00:22:32.710 --> 00:22:33.710
This is your number.

00:22:33.710 --> 00:22:36.360
I'm willing to pay you this, which is more
than this.

